    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish.jpg" alt="Suleiman ibn Qutalmish" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MonumentOfKutalmi%C5%9Fo%C4%9FluS%C3%BCleyman%C5%9EahTarsus.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CeeGee, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sultanate of Rum took its name from the Muslim term for the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, that is, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a>, was a member of the Seljuk family whose father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutalmish">Qutalmish</a> had died rebelling against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> in 1064. After the Byzantine defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> bands spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and rival Byzantine claimants hired them as allies in their civil wars. Suleiman exploited this disorder to win control of much of western and central Anatolia and established himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, only a short distance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, between about 1075 and 1081; the traditional starting date of 1077 is approximate. In 1084 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, but in 1086 he was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Great Seljuk</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-i">Kilij Arslan I</a>, held captive by Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah</a>, was released after Malik-Shah’s death in 1092 and returned to Nicaea. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> took the city in 1097 and defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dorylaeum-1097">Battle of Dorylaeum</a>, pushing the Seljuks back to the central plateau. Kilij Arslan rebuilt his state around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, which became the permanent capital under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesud-i">Mesud I</a>, and in 1101 his army destroyed the crusading expeditions that followed in the wake of the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/konya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konya.jpg" alt="Konya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konyaaltincarsi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Konya Karatay Belediyesi, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate ruled a mixed population. Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> made up a large part of the settled peasantry and townspeople for much of its history, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> pastoralists grazed their herds in the uplands and frontier zones. Over two centuries, migration, intermarriage and conversion gradually shifted the balance towards a Turkish-speaking Muslim society. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksaray">Aksaray</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a> grew as centres of crafts and commerce, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>-like brotherhoods of the <em>akhis</em> organised urban artisans. The sultans’ greatest economic achievement was the trade network of the early thirteenth century. After the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> in 1207, Sinop in 1214 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a> in 1221, Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> ports with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and Italian merchants traded in its harbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultans</a> and officials built more than a hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> along the main roads, among them the great Sultan Han near Aksaray, completed in 1229, which offered lodging, stabling and security to merchants. A fair at Yabanlu Pazarı near Kayseri drew traders from distant regions. The court followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teachers and dervishes found a welcome, and in the 1220s the family of the mystic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Jalal al-Din Rumi</a> settled in Konya.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myriokephalon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-myriokephalon.jpg" alt="Battle of Myriokephalon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myriokephalon">Battle of Myriokephalon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crusades_surprised_by_turks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Seljuk army combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribal cavalry, holders of military land grants (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a></em>), a core of slave soldiers (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a></em>) attached to the sultan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, including Frankish and Byzantine soldiers. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> excelled at ambush and harassment in the rough country of the plateau. The sultanate repeatedly defeated passing crusaders: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-i">Kilij Arslan I</a> destroyed the expeditions of 1101, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesud-i">Mesud I</a>’s forces routed the German army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> in 1147. In 1176 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> trapped and defeated the army of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myriokephalon">Battle of Myriokephalon</a>, ending Byzantine hopes of recovering central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>; two years later he absorbed the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendid emirate</a>. In 1190 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>’s army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-i">Kaykhusraw I</a> was killed in 1211 fighting the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a>, but his sons expanded to the coasts: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykaus-i">Kaykaus I</a> took Sinop and captured the emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a>, building its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-tower-alanya">Red Tower</a> and a shipyard, sent an expedition across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudak">Sudak</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and in 1230 defeated the Khwarazmian ruler Jalal al-Din at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yassicemen">Yassıçemen</a>. The army’s collapse came at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag">Battle of Köse Dağ</a> in 1243, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii.jpg" alt="Kilij Arslan II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Kilij_Arslan_II_(portrait)_in_a_tile_from_Alaeddin_Palace,_Konya,_1156-92.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1156-92 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Seljuk sultans of Rum adopted the court culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Persian was the language of the chancery and of literature, and from the late twelfth century sultans took names from the Persian epic tradition such as Kaykhusraw, Kaykaus and Kayqubad. The sultan ruled with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and a council of great officials, and a bureaucracy of Persian-trained secretaries kept records of taxes and land grants, much of it based on the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a></em> system inherited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Great Seljuks</a>. Princes received provinces as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>, often under a guardian or <em>atabeg</em>, a practice that strengthened dynastic control in good times but encouraged fragmentation: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> divided his lands among his sons, who fought one another after 1190 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-i">Kaykhusraw I</a> reunited the realm. Fortified cities, each with a governor and garrison, anchored royal authority, and the sultans also built palaces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubadabad-palace">Kubadabad</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-beysehir">Lake Beyşehir</a>. Religious law was administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>, while charitable endowments (<em>waqfs</em>) financed mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, hospitals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>. After 1243 power passed to great ministers who governed in the names of child or puppet sultans, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-karatay">Jalal al-Din Karatay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muin-al-din-suleyman-pervane">Mu’in al-Din Süleyman</a>, known as the Pervane, and the vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahib-ata-fakhr-al-din-ali">Fakhr al-Din Ali</a>, called Sahib Ata.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque.jpg" alt="Alaeddin Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque">Alaeddin Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alaedin_Camii.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christian Mathis, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sultanate of Rum laid the foundations of Turkish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Under its rule the peninsula, Greek-speaking and Christian for more than a millennium, began its long transformation into a predominantly Turkish and Muslim land; Western writers were calling it Turchia by the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> left a remarkable architectural legacy in carved stone: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque">Alaeddin Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karatay-madrasa">Karatay Madrasa</a> of 1251, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ince-minareli-madrasa">Ince Minareli Madrasa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gok-madrasa">Gök Madrasa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> and the network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, with their elaborate portals, are among the finest monuments of medieval Islamic architecture. Konya became a centre of Persian literature and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, who died there in 1273, wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a></em>, one of the most influential works of Persian poetry, and his followers formed the Mevlevi order of whirling dervishes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-bibi">Ibn Bibi</a>’s Persian chronicle preserves the history of the dynasty. Politically the sultanate shaped the world that followed it. The Turkish principalities, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a>, that emerged from its collapse, including the Karamanids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, inherited its institutions, artistic models and frontier culture, and Ottoman tradition later claimed that the Seljuk sultan had granted Osman’s family their first lands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag.jpg" alt="Battle of Köse Dağ" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag">Battle of Köse Dağ</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_K%C3%B6z%C3%A4_Dagh_(1243).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown. Catalonia., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> (r. 1220–1237), but its decline was swift. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babai-revolt">Babai revolt</a> of 1239–1240, a rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> led by the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-ishaq">Baba Ishaq</a>, exposed discontent in the countryside, and in 1243 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed the Seljuk army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag">Battle of Köse Dağ</a>. The sultans became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, paying heavy tribute first to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. Rivalry between the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykaus-ii">Kaykaus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-iv">Kilij Arslan IV</a>, each backed by different factions, further weakened the dynasty, and Kaykaus II eventually fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. Real power lay with ministers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muin-al-din-suleyman-pervane">Pervane</a>, whose ambiguous dealings with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluks</a> ended in disaster: in 1277 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> invaded and defeated a Mongol-Seljuk force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a>, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> executed the Pervane and tightened direct Mongol rule. Turkmen leaders on the frontiers, such as the Karamanids, who briefly seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in 1277, built their own principalities. By the 1290s the Seljuk sultans were figureheads appointed and deposed by Mongol governors. The last of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesud-ii">Mesud II</a>, died around 1308, and the sultanate quietly disappeared, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a> from which the Ottoman state would rise.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sultanate of Rum turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, long a Byzantine heartland, towards a Turkish and Muslim future, building a Persianate court culture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, a trade network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and ports, and the institutions and monuments inherited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after Mongol conquest destroyed it.</em></p>

